## Lifecycle and Ownership

A procedure is identified by its schema, name, and the types of its `IN` and `INOUT` arguments; `OUT` arguments are
returned by `CALL` and are not part of the signature. Each argument is one `argument` block, in declaration order.

A `search_path` in `configuration` is a comma-separated list of schema names, each sent as its own literal, because
Redshift reads one quoted literal as a single schema name; any other parameter value is sent as one literal.
`NONATOMIC` excludes `SECURITY DEFINER` and `SET`; such combinations fail validation. The body is sent dollar-quoted and
verbatim; a tag such as `$body$` is chosen when the text contains `$$`.

`owner` is applied with `ALTER PROCEDURE ... OWNER TO`, which requires a superuser. Grant `EXECUTE` separately.
Running a procedure with `CALL` is not part of its lifecycle.

## Reconciliation

Refresh reads the procedure from `pg_proc_info` and its arguments with `SHOW PARAMETERS`; an update runs at most one
`CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE` and one `ALTER PROCEDURE ... OWNER TO`, in that order, and re-reads the catalog to verify
the result; every other change replaces the procedure, because Redshift requires dropping a procedure to change its
signature or output types. `CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE` restates the whole definition, so several changed arguments
share one statement, and it keeps the owner and grants. A replacement runs `DROP PROCEDURE` and then `CREATE PROCEDURE`,
and the new procedure starts without the grants of the old one.

`SHOW PARAMETERS` reports types without their length or precision, so an imported procedure holds bare types such as
`character varying`; a bare type in state matches the same type with a modifier, such as `varchar(64)`.

| Change                                                                                              | Result                                                                     |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| `database`, `schema`, or `name`                                                                     | replaces the procedure: the provider never moves or renames a procedure    |
| `argument` block added, removed, or reordered                                                       | replaces the procedure                                                     |
| `argument.type` respelled with the same canonical type, such as `int4` for `integer`                | no SQL; the state records the configured spelling                          |
| `argument.type` given a length or precision where the state holds the bare type, as after an import | `CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE`, which restates the declared types           |
| `argument.type`, any other change, including another length or precision                            | replaces the procedure, also for an `OUT` argument                         |
| `argument.mode` changed between omitted and `IN`                                                    | no SQL                                                                     |
| `argument.mode`, any other change                                                                   | replaces the procedure                                                     |
| `argument.name` changed only in case                                                                | no SQL; the state records the configured spelling                          |
| `argument.name`, any other change, including a name added or removed                                | replaces the procedure                                                     |
| `argument` blocks known only during apply                                                           | replaces the procedure: the plan cannot tell whether the signature changes |
| `body`                                                                                              | `CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE`                                              |
| `security` changed, or removed while `DEFINER`                                                      | `CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE`; an omitted `security` is `INVOKER`          |
| `nonatomic` set, changed, or removed                                                                | `CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE`, with or without `NONATOMIC`                 |
| `configuration` set, changed, or removed                                                            | `CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE` with the new `SET` clause, or none           |
| `owner` set to a value other than the current owner, or changed                                     | `ALTER PROCEDURE ... OWNER TO`, after any `CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE`    |
| `owner` removed                                                                                     | no SQL; the current owner stays and is still reported                      |

**Drift.** Refresh compares the catalog owner, security mode, body, and argument names, modes, and types with the
state. Redshift stores its own copy of the body, so state keeps the configured `body` while the catalog text still
matches `definition_fingerprint`, a hash recorded at apply time. A body or security mode changed outside Terraform is
restored with `CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE`. A configured `owner` changed outside Terraform is set back with
`ALTER PROCEDURE ... OWNER TO`, and an omitted one follows the catalog. When argument names, modes, or output types no
longer match the catalog, refresh stores the catalog's arguments, and the next plan replaces the procedure; a catalog
argument without a name matches any configured name, because Redshift may keep an `OUT` argument's name only inside the
body. A dropped overload, including one re-created outside Terraform with other input types, is removed from state, and
the next plan creates it again. `nonatomic` and `configuration` are never read back, because Redshift does not report
them: changes made outside Terraform are not detected, and an import leaves both `null`. Lengths and precisions of
argument types are not read back either.

## Import

In Terraform 1.5 and later, use an `import` block with a JSON identity. `arguments` holds the `IN` and `INOUT` types as
`signature` reports them, comma-separated, and is `""` for a procedure without input arguments. They are imported as
`argument` blocks, and the refresh after the import reports argument names, modes, and `OUT` arguments:

